        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> My initial impression is...\r\n\r\nYes, you are right. I even started with another version of your approach.\r\n\r\nWhilst working on the issue, I encountered behavior which I thought to be a bug. Only on the last moment I saw that it was to be [expected](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/0098de999048af0539183d625c52d733318a441b/tests/test_jump.lua#L717), also understanding [this](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/0098de999048af0539183d625c52d733318a441b/tests/test_jump.lua#L520) test.\r\n\r\nBecause of that misperception, I thought I needed to get rid of [this](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/0098de999048af0539183d625c52d733318a441b/lua/mini/jump.lua#L201) early return.\r\n\r\nI submitted the current version because it worked and I think it has two main benefits:\r\n\r\n- Only check if [target](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/0098de999048af0539183d625c52d733318a441b/lua/mini/jump.lua#L200) is present when absolutely necessary\r\n- No need to check if the cursor has changed in order to undo, as that check is a derivative of knowing that a successful jump has occurred. I can schedule a direct undo. \r\n\r\nWould you like me to resubmit with your version?\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n",
